app_dea : Diameter Edge Agent extension for freeDiameter
==========================================================

This extension turns a plain freeDiameter relay into a Diameter Edge Agent
(DEA): a node positioned at the boundary between a trusted (internal)
Diameter network and the outside world (roaming partners, interconnect,
other operators), enforcing privacy, security and routing policy on
messages crossing that boundary.

This is a multi-phase effort. This README tracks what is implemented and
what remains, so the extension can be extended without losing sight of the
full target scope.


Core mechanism (how it hooks into freeDiameter)
------------------------------------------------

freeDiameter routing pipeline, for a message that is not handled locally
(see include/freeDiameter/libfdcore.h, "ROUTING" section):

  received -> routing-in -> [fd_rt_fwd_register callbacks] -> routing-out -> sent

The daemon itself adds a Route-Record AVP for the sending peer BEFORE our
forwarding callback runs (p_psm.c), and routing-out uses Route-Record to
avoid sending a message back the way it came. This means our egress callback
sees the "real" Route-Record chain and can safely rewrite it before the
routing-out logic consumes it.

- `dea_fwd_req` is registered with `RT_FWD_REQ`: runs on every forwarded
  request. This is where masking happens.
- `dea_fwd_ans` is registered with `RT_FWD_ANS`: runs on the matching
  answer. The daemon associates every answer with its request via the
  Hop-by-Hop Id automatically, so `fd_hook_get_request_pmd()` reliably
  retrieves whatever we stashed on the request.
- Request/answer correlation uses `fd_hook_data_register` +
  `fd_hook_get_pmd` / `fd_hook_get_request_pmd` (libfdcore.h). This gives a
  scratch struct (`struct fd_hook_permsgdata`, defined in app_dea.h) that
  lives exactly as long as one transaction. It does NOT persist across a
  whole Diameter session (multiple transactions sharing a Session-Id) --
  see "Phase 2" below for what that requires instead.
- Session-scoped state (Phase 2, Session-Id pairing) uses freeDiameter's own
  session store instead: `fd_msg_sess_get()` resolves/creates a
  `struct session` from a message's Session-Id AVP, and
  `fd_sess_handler_create` + `fd_sess_state_store` / `fd_sess_state_retrieve`
  attach our own data to it (`struct sess_state`, defined in app_dea.h). This
  is a different mechanism from the per-transaction hook data above: it is
  keyed by Session-Id content (not by Hop-by-Hop Id) and survives for the
  configured `session_id_lifetime` of inactivity, i.e. across the whole
  Diameter session, not just one request/answer pair.


Phase 1 -- IMPLEMENTED
------------------------

Topology hiding for the internal -> external direction:

- Peer/realm classification via config (`internal_realm` directives).
  A request is masked only when `Origin-Realm` matches a configured
  internal realm AND `Destination-Realm` does not (fail-safe: anything
  ambiguous, e.g. a missing AVP, is left untouched rather than risk
  mangling legitimate internal traffic).
- `Origin-Host` masking: the real value is saved into the per-transaction
  data and replaced with the configured `hidden_identity` pseudonym.
- `Route-Record` masking: all instances are stripped and replaced with a
  single entry carrying `hidden_identity`, so the DEA itself remains
  attributable without exposing the internal hop chain.
- Config parser (`dea_conf.y` / `dea_conf.l`), following the same
  Flex/Bison pattern as `app_redirect` and `rt_rewrite`.

Deliberately NOT done in Phase 1 (tracked below): Origin-Realm is left
untouched (partners need it for routing/billing -- only node identity is
sensitive, not the operator realm), Session-Id is left untouched (see
Phase 2), CER/CEA capability exchange is left untouched, nothing is
enforced about which Application-Ids are allowed to cross the boundary.


Phase 2 -- Session-Id pseudonymization -- IMPLEMENTED (opt-in)
------------------------------------------------------------------

Session-Id AVPs commonly embed the creating node's Diameter Identity as
their first segment (`DiameterIdentity;hi32;lo32[;opt]`, RFC 6733 §8.8).
Left as-is, this leaks the real internal Origin-Host for the entire session
lifetime, defeating Phase 1's Origin-Host masking on every subsequent
message of the same session. `hide_session_id` (default OFF, see below)
closes this gap: on the first internal->external message of a session, the
Session-Id is replaced with a generated pseudonym, and every later message
of that session -- in both directions, requests and answers -- is
consistently rewritten between the real and masked value.

- **Why opt-in, not default-on like Phase 1**: RFC 6733 expects relay/proxy
  agents to leave Session-Id untouched -- it is meant to stay constant
  end-to-end for session correlation and CDR reconciliation between
  operators. Rewriting it is a deliberate, documented deviation (as used by
  real DEA deployments per GSMA IR.88) made only for topology-hiding
  privacy, not something to silently turn on for every operator.
- **Storage**: reuses freeDiameter's own session store (`fd_sess_*` in
  libfdproto.h) rather than a custom hash table. `fd_msg_sess_get()` on the
  live message resolves the real session; a masked pseudonym session is
  registered with `fd_sess_fromsid()`. Each side stores the *other* side's
  Session-Id string as its `struct sess_state` (`fd_sess_handler_create` /
  `fd_sess_state_store` / `fd_sess_state_retrieve`).
- **Why `fd_sess_fromsid()` and not `fd_sess_new()` to mint the masked
  session**: `fd_sess_new()`'s doc states the new session's initial message
  refcount is 1, released only once a live `struct msg` attaches to it via
  `fd_msg_sess_get()`. Our synthetic masked session has no guarantee of ever
  getting such an attachment on its own, which would pin it in memory
  forever (confirmed by reading `libfdproto/sessions.c` directly, not just
  the header doc). `fd_sess_fromsid()` does not touch that refcount, so we
  generate the RFC-shaped Session-Id string ourselves (mirroring
  `fd_sess_new()`'s own `DiamId;hi32;lo32` format, see `gen_masked_sid()` in
  `dea_hiding.c`) and register it with `fd_sess_fromsid()` instead.
- **Retrieve is destructive**: `fd_sess_state_retrieve()`'s doc says the
  state is detached from the session once retrieved -- a new
  `fd_sess_state_store()` is required to keep it attached. Every read in
  `dea_rewrite_session_id()` immediately stores it back. Without
  synchronization this is a race between two threads handling two messages
  of the same brand-new session concurrently (both could see "no pairing"
  and mint two different masked Session-Ids for the same real session), so
  the whole retrieve-then-restore-or-mint sequence runs under one global
  mutex (`dea_sid_lock`). This is a known Phase 4 scalability limit (it
  serializes Session-Id rewriting across *all* sessions, not just
  same-session concurrent access) -- acceptable for this foundation, flagged
  for revisiting if profiling ever shows contention.
- **Pairing expiry**: both sides of a pairing get their
  `fd_sess_settimeout()` refreshed to the *same* timestamp on every reuse
  (`session_id_lifetime` config, default 86400s of inactivity). Both
  therefore expire together in the same expiry-thread pass. We deliberately
  do NOT cross-destroy the paired session from within the cleanup callback
  (i.e. have session A's cleanup call `fd_sess_destroy()` on session B): the
  reentrancy of that call (B's own cleanup firing while A's is still
  running, immediately trying to destroy A again) could not be fully
  verified as safe by reading `libfdproto/sessions.c` alone, and the
  same-timestamp approach already achieves synchronized expiry without that
  risk.
- **NOT done in Phase 2**: reversible pseudonyms for subscriber identity
  (IMSI, User-Name, Subscription-Id -- see below), and no attempt to detect
  or reject a masked Session-Id being reused/replayed by a malicious peer
  (see Phase 3 fraud detection).


Phase 2.5 -- Subscriber identifier pseudonymization -- IMPLEMENTED (opt-in)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**Scope, clarified with the operator before implementation**: unlike
Origin-Host/Route-Record/Session-Id, a subscriber identifier (IMSI, User-Name)
is NOT masked on the wire towards the external partner -- the partner
legitimately needs the real value to authenticate the subscriber and fetch
subscription data; masking it there would simply break roaming. The actual
need identified was narrower and different: app_dea's own debug logs can
contain PII (compliance concern), but a naive one-way redaction (e.g. hashing
or blanking it) would make debugging a real incident harder (can no longer
tell "these five log lines are about the same subscriber", let alone recover
who it was). So Phase 2.5 is a **log-output-only** feature: it never touches
any AVP, never changes what is sent to any peer.

- **What it does**: `dea_pseudonymize_subscriber_id()` (`dea_pseudonym.c`)
  extracts the subscriber identifier from the (possibly repeated)
  `Subscription-Id` grouped AVP -- the entry whose `Subscription-Id-Type` is
  `END_USER_IMSI` (RFC 4006) -- falling back to `User-Name` if absent, then
  encrypts it with **AES-256-SIV** (RFC 5297, via GnuTLS's AEAD interface,
  `GNUTLS_CIPHER_AES_256_SIV`) under a fixed nonce and hex-encodes the
  result. The token is spliced into the existing `dea_fwd_req` masking log
  line (`app_dea: topology-hid a request for subscriber [<token>] ...`).
  Nothing is written back into the message.
- **Why AES-256-SIV specifically**: it is a *deterministic* authenticated
  cipher -- the same key + plaintext always yields the same ciphertext
  (verified empirically against GnuTLS 3.8.9 before writing the code: same
  input -> same output every time; different input -> different output;
  tampering with a token is detected on decrypt). This directly resolves the
  debuggability-vs-compliance tension: logs stay correlatable by subscriber
  (grep for one token across many log lines) without ever showing the raw
  IMSI, and unlike GCM/CBC, SIV is specifically designed to tolerate the
  fixed/reused nonce this requires (RFC 5297 SS1, "nonce-misuse resistant") --
  reusing a nonce with GCM would be a real vulnerability; with SIV it is the
  intended mode of use.
- **Reversibility, deliberately kept out of the running daemon**: the
  running app_dea process only ever encrypts, never decrypts. Recovering a
  real identifier from a logged token requires the same 64-byte key AND a
  separate, offline tool: `extensions/app_dea/tools/dea_imsi_reveal.c` (not
  built by the extension's CMake, on purpose -- build and run it manually,
  see its header comment; round-trip verified manually before shipping).
  This keeps "can read the logs" and "can de-anonymize a subscriber" as two
  separate privileges, restricted to whoever holds `imsi_pseudonym_key`.
- **Graceful degradation**: `Subscription-Id`/`-Type`/`-Data` come from RFC
  4006 (the `dict_dcca` extension), which may not be loaded; they are
  resolved with `fd_dict_search(..., retval=0)` instead of the `ENOENT`
  pattern used for Phase 1/2's base-protocol AVPs, so a missing dictionary
  degrades to "fall back to User-Name" (logged once as a notice at load
  time) rather than failing extension load.
- **NOT done in Phase 2.5**: no attempt to pseudonymize any other PII-bearing
  AVP (e.g. MSISDN via a different `Subscription-Id-Type`, `Public-Identity`
  in Cx/Dx); no pseudonymization outside `dea_fwd_req`'s existing log line
  (e.g. Phase 3's future fraud-detection audit trail would need its own call
  to `dea_pseudonymize_subscriber_id()`); key rotation is not implemented
  (changing `imsi_pseudonym_key` silently makes all previously logged tokens
  unrecoverable with the new key -- expected for a stateless keyed cipher,
  but worth knowing before rotating in production).


Phase 3 -- Interconnect policy & fraud detection -- IMPLEMENTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Both checks below (`dea_policy.c`) only fire for requests whose sending
realm is NOT one of the configured `internal_realm`(s) -- `dea_fwd_req`
computes `from_internal` once (already needed by Phase 1/2) and gates both
on `!from_internal`, independently of whether `hidden_id` (topology hiding)
is even configured, so an operator can use Phase 3 on its own.

- **Application-Id allow-list for interconnect, per realm** (scope decided
  with the operator: per-partner, not a single global list, to reflect real
  interconnect agreements which differ per partner): `interconnect_realm =
  "<realm>" : <app-id> [<app-id> ...];` (repeatable, `dea_conf.y`/`.l`,
  grammar modeled on `app_redirect`'s rule+target_list pattern). A request's
  `Application-Id` comes from the message header (`struct msg_hdr.msg_appl`,
  not an AVP) and is checked against the rule matching its `Origin-Realm`.
  **Default-deny once active**: as soon as one `interconnect_realm` rule
  exists, any external realm with *no* matching rule is also rejected --
  matching real interconnect practice (only accept traffic from partners you
  have an explicit agreement with), and avoiding the false sense of security
  of "I configured partner A, so I assumed everyone else was blocked too"
  when in fact unconfigured realms would otherwise sail through unrestricted.
  Verified with a standalone test harness linking just the generated
  parser (`dea_conf.tab.c`/`lex.dea_conf.c`) against `libfdproto`, feeding a
  config with multiple realms/app-id lists and asserting the parsed
  structures match (not just "bison reported no grammar conflicts").
- **Fraud detection: Origin-Realm vs. CER/CEA-presented realm**: compares a
  request's `Origin-Realm` AVP against `peer->info.runtime.pir_realm` --
  "the received realm in CER/CEA" for the peer that actually sent us the
  message (`fd_msg_source_get` + `fd_peer_getbyid`), i.e. the realm that
  peer's *connection* was authenticated for, not just what any individual
  message claims. A mismatch is logged at `fd_log_error` (always visible,
  not gated behind `FULL` tracing, since this is security-relevant) and,
  only if `fraud_reject_on_mismatch` is enabled, also rejected.
  **Default is log-only** (`fraud_reject_on_mismatch = 0`): the operator
  enables it explicitly once confident there are no legitimate multi-realm
  peers or other unanticipated cases in their specific network -- avoids the
  check breaking real traffic the first time it is turned on somewhere new.
- **Rejection idiom**: both violations funnel through one `reject_request()`
  helper using the same in-place request -> error-answer conversion as
  `app_redirect`/`rt_rewrite` (`fd_msg_new_answer_from_req` +
  `fd_msg_rescode_set` + `fd_msg_send`), always with
  `DIAMETER_AUTHORIZATION_REJECTED` for both violation types uniformly (not
  a more specific code like `DIAMETER_APPLICATION_UNSUPPORTED`), so a peer
  probing the policy cannot distinguish "wrong Application-Id" from
  "spoofed realm" from the error code alone.
- **NOT done in Phase 3**: rate-limiting/anomaly detection per peer (the
  original idea also included this; descoped to keep this phase to the two
  concretely-specified, immediately actionable checks -- needs its own
  design pass on thresholds/time windows before implementation);
  Route-Record-based consistency checks (Phase 1 already strips/replaces
  Route-Record on egress, so there is little left to cross-check on
  ingress); per-peer Application-Id allow-list as a separate, finer axis
  from per-realm (a peer could in principle serve traffic for a realm
  different from its own -- not handled).
- **CER/CEA / handshake fingerprint leaks**: freeDiameter's Capabilities
  Exchange (product name, firmware revision, supported
  Vendor-Id/Application-Id lists, and IP addresses in Host-IP-Address) is
  handled entirely in libfdcore, before any extension hook runs -- there is
  currently no extension hook point for CER/CEA content. Addressing this
  requires either a core-level change (out of scope for an extension) or
  running a separate stripped-down "mask" identity/instance at the network
  edge. Flagged as a known gap, not solvable purely as an app_dea feature.


Phase 4 -- Operability: HA, observability, performance
----------------------------------------------------------

### Observability -- IMPLEMENTED

`dea_stats.c` adds privacy-aware operational counters: bare integer counts
of event categories (`DEA_STAT_*` in app_dea.h -- requests seen, external
requests, topology-masked, Session-Id minted/reused, subscriber
pseudonymized, interconnect allowed/rejected by reason, fraud mismatches
detected/rejected), never any AVP value or identity, so the dump is safe to
route to any log level or downstream pipeline without re-introducing the
leakage Phase 1/2/2.5 were built to prevent.

- **Mechanism**: follows `dbg_monitor`'s established pattern exactly (same
  extension already in this codebase, same primitives): a background thread
  dumps counters every `stats_interval` seconds (config, default 300s; 0
  disables it), and `fd_event_trig_regcb(SIGUSR2, "app_dea", ...)` triggers
  an immediate dump on demand. SIGUSR2 is the same signal `dbg_monitor` uses
  for its own status dump -- multiple extensions independently registering
  the same trigger value is an established pattern in this codebase (SIGUSR1
  alone is already shared by six other extensions), so both dumps fire
  together if both extensions are loaded.
- **Thread safety**: all counters live behind one `pthread_mutex_t`
  (`dea_stats_lock`), incremented at the exact call sites in `dea_hiding.c`
  / `dea_policy.c` / `dea_pseudonym.c` where the corresponding event already
  happens (no separate observation layer via `fd_hook_register` was needed:
  Phase 1/2/3's own code already sits at every point worth counting).
  Verified empirically, not just reasoned about: a standalone test harness
  ran 8 threads x 10,000 increments each concurrently against the real
  `dea_stats.c` object file (linking only libfdproto + a one-function stub
  for the sole libfdcore symbol it needs, `fd_event_trig_regcb`) and
  asserted the final count was exactly 80,000 -- confirming zero lost
  updates under real concurrent access, not merely "the lock looks correct
  by inspection".
- **NOT done here**: no per-realm/per-peer breakdown of any counter (all
  global process-wide totals); no reset-to-zero capability (counters are
  monotonic for the life of the process); no export format other than the
  log line dump (e.g. no Prometheus/statsd integration) -- see "Performance
  targets" below for why a specific export format isn't picked yet.

### High availability / failover -- NOT STARTED

freeDiameter's own peer/routing-out scoring (`fd_rt_out_register`,
`FD_SCORE_*`) already provides multi-peer failover for the next hop; what's
missing here is DEA-specific state replication -- Phase 2's Session-Id
pairings live in each instance's own in-memory session store, so a failover
mid-session currently loses the mapping (the session gets a fresh masked
pseudonym on its next message, which is safe but changes the identity the
external partner sees mid-session) across redundant DEA instances. Needs a
decision on a shared backend (e.g. Redis, a SQL database like `app_acct`'s
`acct_db.c` already depends on for a different purpose) before
implementation -- a real architecture decision, not started.

### Throttling -- NOT STARTED

Per-peer / per-realm rate limiting -- the rate-limiting / anomaly-detection
half of the original Phase 3 idea, descoped from Phase 3 itself (see there)
since it needs its own design pass on thresholds and time windows, same as
Phase 3's fraud check needed a log-vs-reject decision before implementation.

### Testing -- NOT STARTED

Fuzzing the config parser and the AVP-rewrite paths (malformed/oversized
AVPs, repeated Route-Record floods, missing mandatory AVPs), plus dictionary
coverage checks so masking rules don't silently no-op when a dictionary
extension isn't loaded. Different in kind from the other Phase 4 items (a
QA activity, not a runtime feature).

### Performance targets -- BLOCKED

Not yet defined; needs a number (msgs/sec, p99 added latency) from whoever
owns the deployment before it can drive design choices -- Phase 2's single
global `dea_sid_lock` (see above) is the first thing to revisit if a target
requires it, and it's also what would decide whether Observability's
counters need a lower-overhead export path than periodic log lines.


Known limitations of the current code
----------------------------------------

- Origin-Host, Route-Record, and (opt-in) Session-Id are masked; anything
  else carrying topology information (Proxy-Info, vendor-specific AVPs with
  embedded hostnames) passes through unchanged -- see Phase 2.5/3.
- Masking runs on every internal->external request unconditionally once
  configured; there is no per-peer opt-out yet (e.g. a partner you
  deliberately don't hide topology from).
- All Session-Id rewriting is serialized behind one global mutex
  (`dea_sid_lock` in `dea_hiding.c`) -- correct but not scalable past
  whatever throughput that single critical section can sustain; see Phase 4.
- Session-Id pairings are process-local (no replication across redundant DEA
  instances) and are not persisted across a restart of the extension/daemon;
  see Phase 4 (HA).
- `dea_fwd_ans` handles Session-Id un/re-masking but otherwise only logs for
  Origin-Host/Route-Record; it is the intended integration point for
  Phase 3 features that need to act further on the answer path.
- Phase 2.5's subscriber pseudonymization is wired into exactly one log line
  (`dea_fwd_req`'s masking path); any future logging that touches subscriber
  identity (e.g. a Phase 3 fraud-detection audit trail) needs its own call
  to `dea_pseudonymize_subscriber_id()`, it is not automatic.
- `imsi_pseudonym_key` has no rotation support: it is one static key for the
  life of the process, read once at config parse time.
- Phase 3's interconnect allow-list is per-realm, not per-peer: a peer
  serving traffic for a realm different from its own is not distinguished.
- Phase 3's fraud check only compares Origin-Realm against the sending
  peer's CER/CEA realm; it does not rate-limit, does not build any anomaly
  baseline over time, and does not look at any AVP besides Origin-Realm
  (e.g. Origin-Host is not cross-checked against the peer's Diameter-Id).
